WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.669
Continuity means the output at an input matches the height approached nearby

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:12.254
Three things are needed: a defined value, an existing two-sided limit, and agreement between them

00:00:12.254 --> 00:00:15.767
A graph without a break illustrates this relationship.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:22.972
Now keep the nearby line but move only the value at one up to five

00:00:22.972 --> 00:00:25.728
The approaching outputs still converge to two

00:00:25.728 --> 00:00:31.633
A single misplaced value makes the function discontinuous even though its nearby behavior is unchanged.

00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:37.962
Setting the value at one equal to two repairs this removable discontinuity

00:00:37.962 --> 00:00:42.152
This works because both sides already have the same limit

00:00:42.152 --> 00:00:47.600
Merely defining a value would not repair a jump with unequal one-sided limits.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.223
Continuity also respects the domain

00:00:51.223 --> 00:00:55.405
The square root function accepts only nonnegative real inputs

00:00:55.405 --> 00:01:04.233
At its endpoint zero, continuity uses approach from within that domain, so only the right-hand approach is relevant there.
